{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/264887-orpheus-questions-from-a-first-time-orpheus-st","id":264887,"title":"[Orpheus] Questions from a First-Time Orpheus ST","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-24T14:32:58-07:00","id":264887,"title":"[Orpheus] Questions from a First-Time Orpheus ST","content":"I would like advice on running <b>Orpheus</b>. It's my first time running any classic World of Darkness game; I've only run nWoD before now. I read the core rulebook a couple times over the two weeks, and a few years ago I read all of the supplements. I've even read the novel, <b>Haunting the Dead</b>.<br /> <br /> <b><u>I plan to run the entire six-book campaign. </u></b><br /> First, I'm going to run several stories before Orpheus falls. During these initial stories, I have two goals. First, get the players comfortable with the structure of Orpheus Group, so that they feel the loss of the support structure when things go to hell. Second, I want to establish the conspiracies that make up the game, and give the players hooks that they can look into once Orpheus Group is no longer holding their leashes. I'm concerned that the latter could be a major task: I want to make sure that when the Crusade of Ashes begins, the players aren't adrift, confused about what to do and where to go, but I don't want them to jump the gun on conspiracies.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u>House Rules</u></b><br /> I am adopting the Orpheus errata collected by Sean Smith and J. Edward Tremlett, based on questions and e-mails with Lucien Soulban, C. A. Suleiman and Matt McFarland.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u>A Word on Spite</u></b><br /> Spite, despite being one of the most important concepts, is poorly explained. Here are some questions that never get appropriately answered. <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Do Spite points go from 0-9? Or 1-10? The core rulebook, and some early errata, suggest the former. <b>Shadow Games</b> has a chapter on Spite, which is inconsistent in itself. It initially mentions the 0-9 scale, but in the very next sentence begins describing Spite with the 1-10 model, and uses that model in an example: a character's Spite points rolled back, and his Spite increased, because he got up to 11 Spite points.</li> <li>How often can a character tap each Spite point? Or to put another point, when does a tapped Spite point refresh?</li> <li>Can you tap your Spite points and your Spite rating in the same interval? Or is it a one-or-the-other deal?</li> </ol><br /> <br /> I discussed the Spite mechanic with my players. Here's what the players wanted. The default rules are in play, with the following exceptions... <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Every lament, except hues, begin play with one Spite point. When a character hits a new Spite rating, their Spite point total returns to &quot;one&quot;.</li> <li>Hues begin play with either one Spite point, or a number of Spite points equal to the Spite gained from their Shade, whichever is greater. When a hue hits a new Spite rating, their Spite point total returns to that number.</li> <li>In a given turn, characters can tap each of their Spite points once, and their Spite rating once. Spite points/rating refresh at the end of the turn.</li> </ol><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3934,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3934-fetch","user":"Fetch","date":". 09-24-2014, 11:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3934-fetch","name":"Fetch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3934_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}